WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.260
A diode problem is really a circuit problem plus a consistency check.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.215
Use a stated zero point seven volt forward drop model, with reverse leakage

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:11.380
and

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:12.200
breakdown neglected.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.800
Connect a source through one kilo ohm to an output node.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.360
From that node to ground, place a ten kilo ohm load and a diode in parallel.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.240
Its anode is at the node and cathode at ground.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.640
Start with a positive five volt source.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.800
If the diode were off, the divider would give about four point five five volts

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.300
at the node.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.980
That would forward bias our diode, so off is inconsistent.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.620
With the diode on, output is zero point seven volts.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.530
The series resistor current is five minus zero point seven, divided by one

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.580
thousand, or four point three milliamperes.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.000
But does all that current enter the diode?

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.880
Pause and check the load branch.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.853
The load takes zero point seven divided by ten thousand amperes, or zero point

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.100
zero seven milliamperes.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.160
Kirchhoff's current law leaves four point two three milliamperes through the

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.500
diode, in its allowed direction.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.540
Now change the source to negative zero point five volts.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.520
An on assumption would require a negative forward diode current, so reject it.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.682
With the diode off, the divider gives output negative zero point four five

00:01:12.682 --> 00:01:12.888
four

00:01:12.888 --> 00:01:13.960
five volts approximately.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.560
That reverse biases the diode consistently.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.020
We did not simply clamp every output to zero point seven regardless of input.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.140
Label the diode orientation, assume a state, solve the remaining circuit, and

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.140
verify the resulting voltage and current.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.980
Real diode curves are smooth and temperature dependent; zero point seven is

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.340
only the chosen approximation for this exercise.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.620
Any practical circuit also needs current limits and component ratings.

00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:44.192
A Zener does not hold its rated voltage under every possible load.

00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:48.412
In the simplified regulator, a series resistor supplies a parallel combination

00:01:48.412 --> 00:01:50.912
of the reverse biased Zener and the load.

00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.592
Use Zener voltage five point one volts, series resistance three hundred thirty

00:01:55.592 --> 00:01:58.052
ohms and load resistance one kilo ohm.

00:01:58.672 --> 00:02:02.692
At twelve volts input, series current is six point nine divided by three

00:02:02.692 --> 00:02:06.172
hundred thirty, or about twenty point nine zero nine milliamperes.

00:02:06.532 --> 00:02:09.172
The load takes five point one milliamperes.

00:02:09.172 --> 00:02:12.752
Zener current is the difference, about fifteen point eight zero nine

00:02:12.752 --> 00:02:13.352
milliamperes.

00:02:14.352 --> 00:02:18.412
Suppose our specified usable Zener window is five to twenty five milliamperes.

00:02:19.032 --> 00:02:20.732
The nominal point is inside it.

00:02:21.192 --> 00:02:25.672
To keep that same load regulated, minimum input is five point one plus three

00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:29.032
hundred thirty times the sum of five milliamperes and load current.

00:02:29.572 --> 00:02:31.432
That gives eight point four three three volts.

00:02:32.032 --> 00:02:35.972
The upper current limit gives maximum input fifteen point zero three three

00:02:35.972 --> 00:02:36.272
volts.

00:02:36.272 --> 00:02:40.272
These bounds belong to this fixed load, constant Zener voltage model.

00:02:41.072 --> 00:02:44.392
Now replace the load by two hundred twenty ohms while keeping twelve volts

00:02:44.392 --> 00:02:44.792
input.

00:02:45.472 --> 00:02:49.080
At a supposed five point one volts, the load would demand twenty three point

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.452
one eight milliamperes, more than the series resistor supplies.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:56.172
A negative computed Zener current tells us regulation failed.

00:02:57.072 --> 00:03:01.172
Neglecting leakage below breakdown, the off Zener divider gives output four

00:03:01.172 --> 00:03:02.332
point eight volts instead.

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:06.712
Never report five point one volts just because that number is printed beside

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:07.212
the device.

00:03:08.072 --> 00:03:12.240
At the nominal regulated point, Zener power is approximately zero point zero

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.472
eight zero six watts.

00:03:14.292 --> 00:03:16.472
Resistor power and device ratings also need checking.

00:03:17.212 --> 00:03:20.612
Real knee currents, tolerances and thermal effects change the bounds.

00:03:21.512 --> 00:03:25.872
JEE's clean routine is current balance, operating window check, and power

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:26.712
check, in that order.

00:03:26.712 --> 00:03:29.892
Let's move from current paths to logical states.

00:03:30.672 --> 00:03:33.812
We use ideal binary inputs zero and one.

00:03:34.712 --> 00:03:36.672
A NAND output is the negation of AND.

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:42.732
Tie its two inputs together and you obtain NOT: NAND of A with A is not A.

00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:44.432
Do the same to B.

00:03:45.372 --> 00:03:47.692
Feed those two inverted signals into a third NAND.

00:03:48.272 --> 00:03:51.252
Its result is not of not A AND not B.

00:03:51.252 --> 00:03:54.252
De Morgan's identity turns that into A OR B.

00:03:55.072 --> 00:03:58.572
Rather than trusting a familiar looking symbol, build its complete table.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:03.872
For inputs zero zero, the first two outputs are one one, and the final NAND

00:04:03.872 --> 00:04:04.492
gives zero.

00:04:05.152 --> 00:04:08.392
For zero one, they are one zero, and output is one.

00:04:09.072 --> 00:04:11.152
For one zero, output is also one.

00:04:11.672 --> 00:04:15.572
For one one, the intermediate pair is zero zero, and output is one.

00:04:15.932 --> 00:04:19.292
The resulting sequence is zero, one, one, one: OR.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:22.252
Now use three NOR gates in the analogous pattern.

00:04:22.972 --> 00:04:25.092
A tied input NOR also makes NOT.

00:04:25.812 --> 00:04:30.092
The final NOR gives not of not A OR not B, which is A AND B.

00:04:30.692 --> 00:04:32.892
Its table is zero, zero, zero, one.

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:37.372
Pause and predict the all one input before checking: both inverted signals

00:04:37.372 --> 00:04:39.792
become zero, and the last NOR gives one.

00:04:40.392 --> 00:04:43.752
These constructions explain why NAND and NOR are called universal gates.

00:04:44.472 --> 00:04:48.392
More complicated networks can be simplified with Boolean algebra, but checking

00:04:48.392 --> 00:04:52.112
all combinations remains an excellent safeguard for a small network.

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:57.772
Do not use ordinary arithmetic addition for OR: one OR one is one, not two.

00:04:58.312 --> 00:05:02.612
These are ideal logic models with stable defined inputs; practical voltage

00:05:02.612 --> 00:05:06.206
thresholds, delays and floating input behaviour require a device

00:05:06.206 --> 00:05:06.992
specification.

00:05:07.752 --> 00:05:12.412
For JEE, annotate the intermediate signals and test the full table, especially

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:14.892
any inversion bubbles that are easy to overlook.
